+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Use :func:`~rich.progress.open` to read a file while a progress bar shows how many bytes have been read. It accepts the same ``mode``, ``buffering``, ``encoding``, ``errors``, and ``newline`` arguments as the built-in ``open``. The default mode is ``'r'``, which returns a text reader, so ``read()`` returns a ``str``. Use ``'rb'`` to get a binary reader, where ``read()`` returns ``bytes``. The default ``total`` is the size of the file in bytes. Here's an example::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.txt") as f:
+        text = f.read()
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+If you already have a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, use its :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` method instead. It adds a task (or updates the task you pass with ``task_id``) and returns a reader without starting the display, so you can manage the display yourself::
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        with progress.open("data.bin", "rb", description="Loading") as f:
+            data = f.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file-like object, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file`. It returns a reader that advances the progress bar as bytes are read. Only binary file objects are supported. You must supply ``total``, the size of the data in bytes, unless you pass a task that already has a total. The reader does not close the wrapped file; the caller still owns it. Here's an example::
+
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as source:
+        with wrap_file(source, 1024) as f:
+            data = f.read()
+
+With a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, use its :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` method. It can reuse an existing task, in which case the task's total is used when ``total`` is not given::
+
+    import io
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    payload = b"example data"
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        task_id = progress.add_task("Parsing", total=len(payload))
+        f = progress.wrap_file(io.BytesIO(payload), task_id=task_id)
+        data = f.read()
+
